ns and the  ripeness of those beans is going to be  completely different  and that's why colombian coffee became  so famous  because the topography that the rest of  latin america has which is these  really rough mountains allows for the  right elevation for coffee to grow but  it doesn't allow for mechanization so  you can't have mechanical picking here  everything has to be done by hand and  what the latin american countries did is  they capitalized on what would have  otherwise been  a disadvantage right so instead of  having these  very efficient plantations as they do in  brazil what these countries did was  promote the quality of their coffee  however as i mentioned everything has to  be done by hand so  labor is incredibly important in  coffee production and these are actual  pictures  of last year's harvest in colombia from  this  um great article but i just want to  point out that  the ripeness of the cherries is very  even what you can see in the basket in  the top left  is that all the beans are red and ripe  and there's actually some farms where  the pickers will  paint their thumb nails red so that they  can match up  the thumbnails to the cherries that  they're picking  so this is the organization that we  worked with through the smart programs  their name is federacion campesina del  cauca  and they are a  second tier organization that makes up  around 700 member families  they are all small holders which means  that they  have relatively small farms the average  farm size  in this organization is around maybe  three acres  or 1.5 hectares something like that  they promote organic coffee as part of  their mission to improve the livelihood  of local farmers  and they're just they're a wonderful  group and that they have really tried to  do  all of it they try to work on the  growing part of it on the quality part  of it on the marketing  maybe even started roasting their own  coffee  and part of the the biggest lesson that  i got from them  is that cof