tion  in terms of flowering and  um an interesting fact it's an aloe  tetraploid which means that it has  it the species arose as a hybridization  event between  coffea canefera which is another coffee  crop that we actually consume  and coffee euglenoides which is not a  crop  and the result kofi arabica  kept two complete copies of both of  those genomes  um it is the only polyploid species in  the genus  it is self-pollinating which makes it  relatively easy  to propagate and each flower will  produce a droop  which is what we call the coffee cherry  and then those have  usually have two seeds which is what we  call the coffee beans and that's what  most people are familiar with it's just  a very slow crop to grow so most farmers  will start their plants from seed  and it'll take them six months between  sowing to actually transplanting them in  the field  you won't get a first harvest until two  years after  and then each year after that you'll get  an inflorescence which takes nine months  to develop into a fully mature seed  so it is it is actually a really slowly  developing crop and it also means that  as a farmer you need to take care of  these seeds  for a really long time before you can  harvest them  each plant can live around 20 years but  more intensive  management styles will place plants  around every eight to ten years  so like i said coffee grows in the  tropics because of it  the ideal temperature ranges  and brazil is the only country that can  afford to grow it on flat land  so they have these huge extensions of  perfect altitude perfect temperature  and this has allowed them to have  enormous plantations that are completely  mechanized and completely  everything is done in a really  technological way so you see  monocultures  all the same variety everything is  handled in a very intensive way  but as you can see in the image to the  left that also leads to  harvesting that isn't even so you are  harvesting green beans you're harvesting  red beans and the  ripeness of those beans is going to be  completely different  and that's why colombian coffee became  so famous  because the topography that the rest of  latin america has which is these  really rough mountains allows for the  right elevation for coffee to grow but  it doesn't allow for mechanization so  you can't have mechanical picking here  everything has to be done by hand and  what the latin american countries did is  they capitalized on what would have  otherwise been  a disadvantage right so instead of  having these  very efficient plantations as they do in  brazil what these countries did was  promote the quality of their coffee  however as i mentioned everything has to  be done by hand so  labor is incredibly important in  coffee production and these are actual  pictures  of last year's harvest in colombia from  this  um great article but i just want to  point out that  the ripeness of the cherries is very  even what you can see in the basket in  the top left  is that all the beans are red and ripe  and there's actually some farms where  the pickers will  paint their thumb nails red so that they  can match up  the thumbnails to the cherries that  they're picking  so this is the organization that we  worked with through the smart programs  their name is federacion campesina del  cauca  and they are a  second tier organization that makes up  around 700 member families  they are all small holders which means  that they  have relatively small farms the average  farm size  in this organization is around maybe  three acres  or 1.5 hectares something like that  they promote organic coffee as part of  their mission to improve the livelihood  of local farmers  and they're just they're a wonderful  group and that they have really tried to  do  all of it they try to work on the  growing part of it on the quality part  of it on the marketing  maybe even started roasting their own  coffee  and part of the the biggest lesson that  i got from them  is that coffee growers they know what  they're doing they know how to manage  their farms and they know  how to make the right decisions for  their farms the one thing that they do  not have  access to is the market side of it  so they do have a lot of other  challenges and  climate change is just exacerbating all  of them and it is incredible to see  how aware these farmers are of climate  change  there isn't a single farmer i've ever  talked to who doesn't mention climate  change as the biggest challenge they  have  and one of the consequences of this is  that the coffee berry borer which is  this tiny little beetle  um it likes hot temperatures and it  benefits  from hotter years in that it manages to  produce more and more offspring and have  basically it accelerates its life cycle  so you have more generations happening  within  a particular time frame and this is a  picture of  my hand holding a bean that was infested  with them  um their life cycle is very interesting  that they very  infrequently mate most of their  reproduction happens through  parasitogenesis so the females will  leave the infected being only for a very  short period of time  and will find an uninfected being and  they bore their  way into it lay eggs and then die inside  the bean  the picture on the right here is a trap  where you have soapy water in the bottom  of this bottle and then in the little  vial you have  a mixture of ethanol and methanol which  attracts them  and they're not very smart they just  fall in there and die  but the they use these traps to keep  track of the population  sizes and the only way to manage this  bug really  is cultural so you have to pick every  single bean  that is in the farm if you let any beans  fall to the ground they will just become  breeding grounds for new bugs  because they spend such a short amount  of time outside of the bean  spraying for them is not really an  option  so unless your neighbors and everyone in  your region is working  together to control this bug it can  really become a problem  if people leave  ripe beans just lying around for them to  infest  another huge issue that coffee growers  have is coffee  leaf rust which is a fungus it is a  obligate um oh my god for another word  they need a living tissue to reproduce  so they will infect the underside of the  leaves  and they create these little spots that  look like rust hence the name  and they can propagate ridiculously long  distances and it's really hard to  understand what is  exactly going to cause an outbreak of  rest so it's very hard to control  um there were two particular cases that  kind of stand out  in the history of coffee rust in latin  america  in 2008 it devastated colombia and then  in 2011  to 2012 it devastated central america  and they think that  the patterns kind of match up with  greater  metal meteorological patterns so they  think that el nino  fluctuations might be involved in  spreading the spores throughout  the whole region there's also  a lot of influence on the changes  between daylight and nighttime  temperatures  and relative humidity so unfortunately  climate change is just exacerbating the  disease pressure on coffee plants and a  lot of what  um conventional coffee plants  coffee plantations will have is  susceptible varieties  and as you can see on the right what  ends up happening  is when you have a really bad  infestation your plants lose all their  leaves  they don't necessarily die from it but  your crop is definitely affected for the  rest of the year  working with the fct i learned though  that the biggest problem  that coffee growers have is the  fluctuations in the coffee price  because most of the coffee in the world  is still  being sold through the stock exchange  and that's terribly unfortunate because  the stock exchange doesn't really care  about the weather  or how good the production was in one  place versus another so  because of all the financial tools that  people use  trading and futures and