nment  and land use substitution  so you've probably all heard about the  south american  the central american migrations that are  happening people are  afraid and there's a lot of violence  there's very  very few economic opportunities so  people are leaving  their land in the case of colombia  there's huge pressure from  illegal crops on the same lands that  coffee now  occupies there's also been a lot of  expansion of range  into higher elevations because of  climate change so as temperatures get  higher  people are pushing higher higher up the  mountains threatening  native forests there is also a couple of  countries that had traditionally banned  the growing of coffee  coneferra known as robusta because it's  traditionally known as lower quality  coffee  but they are now starting to allow it  and there's also a lot of urbanization  as  some of these communities get more  wealthy people start leaving the  countryside for the city they decide  to build houses on their farms and so  all of this land is being lost  this problem is particularly bad in  costa rica  i'm going to focus on these three trends  just because  i saw them during my junior trip  um and because i think they're the more  general ones  to the whole of latin america so  there's a huge problem with traditional  coffee varieties and that they are  a genetic bottleneck  and there's absolutely no disease  resistance genes in them so  most of what we know as  at least arabica coffee comes from the  few plants  that the europeans back in the  probably 1600s  took from yemen and started bringing to  their colonies  in particular to indonesia and to it  with ceylon  and that in itself is already a  bottleneck but then the plants that got  to latin america  are just a substitute of that  so all of the varieties that are now  grown  throughout all of latin america come  basically  from the typica and the bourbon  and even though there are a handful of  other and people usually  will associate the diversi