tion  in terms of flowering and  um an interesting fact it's an aloe  tetraploid which means that it has  it the species arose as a hybridization  event between  coffea canefera which is another coffee  crop that we actually consume  and coffee euglenoides which is not a  crop  and the result kofi arabica  kept two complete copies of both of  those genomes  um it is the only polyploid species in  the genus  it is self-pollinating which makes it  relatively easy  to propagate and each flower will  produce a droop  which is what we call the coffee cherry  and then those have  usually have two seeds which is what we  call the coffee beans and that's what  most people are familiar with it's just  a very slow crop to grow so most farmers  will start their plants from seed  and it'll take them six months between  sowing to actually transplanting them in  the field  you won't get a first harvest until two  years after  and then each year after that you'll get  an inflorescence which takes nine months  to develop into a fully mature seed  so it is it is actually a really slowly  developing crop and it also means that  as a farmer you need to take care of  these seeds  for a really long time before you can  harvest them  each plant can live around 20 years but  more intensive  management styles will place plants  around every eight to ten years  so like i said coffee grows in the  tropics because of it  the ideal temperature ranges  and brazil is the only country that can  afford to grow it on flat land  so they have these huge extensions of  perfect altitude perfect temperature  and this has allowed them to have  enormous plantations that are completely  mechanized and completely  everything is done in a really  technological way so you see  monocultures  all the same variety everything is  handled in a very intensive way  but as you can see in the image to the  left that also leads to  harvesting that isn't even so you are  harvesting green beans you're harvesting  red beans and the  ripeness of those beans is going to be  completely different  and that's why colombian coffee became  so famous  because the topography that the rest of  latin america has which is these  really rough mountains allows for the  right elevation for coffee to grow but  it doesn't allow for mechanization so  you can't have mechanical picking here  everything has to be done by hand and  what the latin american countries did is  they capitalized on what would have  otherwise been  a disadvantage right so instead of  having these  very efficient plantations as they do in  brazil what these countries did was  promote the quality of their coffee  however as i mentioned everything has to  be done by hand so  labor is incredibly important in  coffee production and these are actual  pictures  of last year's harvest in colombia from  this  um great article but i just want to  point out that  the ripeness of the cherries is very  even what you can see in the basket in  the top left  is that all the beans are red and ripe  and there's actually some farms where  the pickers will  paint their thumb nails red so that they  can match up  the thumbnails to the cherries that  they're picking  so this is the organization that we  worked with through the smart programs  their name is federacion campesina del  cauca  and they are a  second tier organization that makes up  around 700 member families  they are all small holders which means  that they  have relatively small farms the average  farm size  in this organization is around maybe  three acres  or 1.5 hectares something like that  they promote organic coffee as part of  their mission to improve the livelihood  of local farmers  and they're just they're a wonderful  group and that they have really tried to  do  all of it they try to work on the  growing part of it on the quality part  of it on the marketing  maybe even started roasting their own  coffee  and part of the the biggest lesson that  i got from them  is that cof