l this bug it can  really become a problem  if people leave  ripe beans just lying around for them to  infest  another huge issue that coffee growers  have is coffee  leaf rust which is a fungus it is a  obligate um oh my god for another word  they need a living tissue to reproduce  so they will infect the underside of the  leaves  and they create these little spots that  look like rust hence the name  and they can propagate ridiculously long  distances and it's really hard to  understand what is  exactly going to cause an outbreak of  rest so it's very hard to control  um there were two particular cases that  kind of stand out  in the history of coffee rust in latin  america  in 2008 it devastated colombia and then  in 2011  to 2012 it devastated central america  and they think that  the patterns kind of match up with  greater  metal meteorological patterns so they  think that el nino  fluctuations might be involved in  spreading the spores throughout  the whole region there's also  a lot of influence on the changes  between daylight and nighttime  temperatures  and relative humidity so unfortunately  climate change is just exacerbating the  disease pressure on coffee plants and a  lot of what  um conventional coffee plants  coffee plantations will have is  susceptible varieties  and as you can see on the right what  ends up happening  is when you have a really bad  infestation your plants lose all their  leaves  they don't necessarily die from it but  your crop is definitely affected for the  rest of the year  working with the fct i learned though  that the biggest problem  that coffee growers have is the  fluctuations in the coffee price  because most of the coffee in the world  is still  being sold through the stock exchange  and that's terribly unfortunate because  the stock exchange doesn't really care  about the weather  or how good the production was in one  place versus another so  because of all the financial tools that  people use  trading and futures and