 teamed up with these four  organizations  one was in mexico in honduras in  colombia which happens to be the fcc the  organization i worked with  and then one in peru and between them  they gathered 2  400 members that their average farm size  was 3.5 hectares so that's  somewhere around 8 acres  this represents a total volume of more  than 16 million pounds which is a lot of  coffee  most of it is certified fair trade and  most of it is organic so these farmers  actually have access to what we would  consider the higher  end of the coffee price  however what this study found was that  none of these farmers are making enough  money to make their farms viable in the  long term  this graph shows the cost of coffee  production  separated from in in different  categories  so the variable costs are the things  that you don't necessarily have to do  every time  things like you might fertilize this  year but not next year  or you may have to invest more in  pesticides  the fixed costs are the things that  happen every year things like taxes  labor for the harvest the depreciation  cost  includes the cost of okay so you buy a  tractor this year and then  it wears and tears over a year so it  loses value  and then the total costs  account for how much it costs to  establish  the coffee farm in the in the in the  first place how much it costs to buy  new seeds things like that that don't  necessarily  happen all the time but that ultimately  make  make part of your of your agricultural  business  and then the little red dot in the  middle is the farm gate price that these  coffee growers are getting so even  though  most of these people were selling their  coffee as fair trade certified which  would guarantee them that 1.40 minimum  they are not getting that they are  getting a ridiculously lower price  so part of this experience that i've had  with coffee  is meeting all of these small holder  farmers  and around the world we're talking about  25 million people who  farm less than 10 hectares and  usually fall below the poverty line  but many of them have this incredibly  strong attachment to their land and  their culture they've been farming  coffee for generations  um many can trace their coffee ancestry  back to like the 1800s at least the  people i've spoken to in colombia  so losing their coffee business  is more than just losing their cash crop  it's very tied to their identity  and because they are farmers they're  also growing their own food so even  though you don't necessarily put a value  on the additional crops that you have  you're still feeding your family off of  that so the value that is  in a way not um monetized  is is incredible and it's at risk of  being lost  so um i used part of the time on the  drear to participate in this  workshop that was organized by the  university of wagnerhead  in collaboration with siat which is a  center for  international tropical agriculture based  in kali colombia  and as you can see many of you might  recognize alyssa pritz and marie  swetzland who are cornelians  they brought together these coffee  researchers from  around the world with different  expertise on  pests and on marketing and on  agroforestry systems and they wanted to  diagnose what is going on with coffee  growing and what is going to happen  to all of these people and all these  um landscapes that are changing  and we identified six  main trends and research needs  um for the next decade  um so the first thing that we're seeing  is that  people are substituting their  traditional varieties because like i  said traditional varieties are very  susceptible to rust  so if you can have a resistant variety  you are going to simplify  your production costs but also it's  going to make your whole farm a little  bit  more resilient there's also a big push  towards intensification because  if you can't reduce your costs you have  to increase your yields  to be to keep financially sustainable  there's also a lot of land abandonment  and land use substitution  so you've probably all heard about the  south american  the central american migrations that are  happening people are  afraid and there's a lot of violence  there's very  very few economic opportunities so  people are leaving  their land in the case of colombia  there's huge pressure from  illegal crops on the same lands that  coffee now  occupies there's also been a lot of  expansion of range  into higher elevations because of  climate change so as temperatures get  higher  people are pushing higher higher up the  mountains threatening  native forests there is also a couple of  countries that had traditionally banned  the growing of coffee  coneferra known as robusta because it's  traditionally known as lower quality  coffee  but they are now starting to allow it  and there's also a lot of urbanization  as  some of these communities get more  wealthy people start leaving the  countryside for the city they decide  to build houses on their farms and so  all of this land is being lost  this problem is particularly bad in  costa rica  i'm going to focus on these three trends  just because  i saw them during my junior trip  um and because i think they're the more  general ones  to the whole of latin america so  there's a huge problem with traditional  coffee varieties and that they are  a genetic bottleneck  and there's absolutely no disease  resistance genes in them so  most of what we know as  at least arabica coffee comes from the  few plants  that the europeans back in the  probably 1600s  took from yemen and started bringing to  their colonies  in particular to indonesia and to it  with ceylon  and that in itself is already a  bottleneck but then the plants that got  to latin america  are just a substitute of that  so all of the varieties that are now  grown  throughout all of latin america come  basically  from the typica and the bourbon  and even though there are a handful of  other and people usually  will associate the diversity in the bean  sizes and colors which you can see in  the left hand side  with genetic diversity the truth is that  these varieties are  very similar to each other so  there's been a huge push for  promoting these varieties called  katimores and sarchimores  because they come from a wild hybrid  that was found  years ago decades ago in indonesia  in an island called timur which is a  hybrid between kofia arabica  and kofia kanaphara and that hybrid  actually has disease resistance genes  against  coffee leaf rest so people have been  introgressing those genes  back into the more culturally acceptable  coffee varieties  but there's been a lot of pushback  because they are associated with lower  quality products  so like i mentioned before there was a  big uh leaf rust outbreak  in colombia in 2008  where 80 of the coffee was susceptible  to rust  and since then the federation of coffee  growers in colombia has been pushing  really hard  to get people to plant resistant  varieties to the point where in 2018  80 of the coffee in columbia was  resistant to rust  so at least that problem has been kind  of taken care of  however in speaking with the farmers  i've learned that  they don't particularly oops they don't  particularly like  growing these resistant varieties  because they have other characteristics  they're harder to pick  um the beans feel different the sizes  are different  and there's this myth that the quality  is inferior  i do want to take a second to um talk  about  the national coffee grove federation in  colombia it's basically it started as a  union of coffee growers  and i want to mention them because  they are the only example i know of  where there's a strong institution that  is taking care of coffee growers in the  region  and you can probably recognize the logo  they did a incredible marketing campaign  back in the  80s where something like 90 percent of  people in europe will recognize the logo  as a high quality product  and the image in the left is an actual  actor who's this is his real-time  job he is juan valdez he impersonates  this  colombian coffee grower and he carries  his meal around  this is at the um  the red carpet party that they held for  a movie  a while ago called bruce almighty where  he actually  requests coffee grown in the mountains  of colombia from juan valdez  but the reason i bring it up is because  it's  probably the most successful marketing  campaign for  an agricultural product that doesn't yet  have any added  value like colombian coffee is green  beans it's not even roasted  and yet everyone everywhere recognizes  it as a high quality product  in fact i learned recently that in 2005  it was the first  non-european product to be granted  a protected denomination of origin  so there's essentially champagne and  parmesan cheese  and then there's colombian coffee which  is really impressive  so there's been another trend in how  coffee is grown uh it used to be that  coffee was grown  in the understory of a forest that's  where the coffee plants come from  and so you can have what is known as  rustic coffee where you just have an  intact forest that you just grow a  couple of coffee plants on the bottom of  but it's there's been a strong push  towards intensification which is on the  on the lower end of this figure  where you basically have no shade or  just a few  shade trees  and this comes in part because if you're  in the  mountains usually you have a lot of rain  so your plants are going to be light  limited  and if you want to increase your yields  the best thing you can do is increase  the light  access of your plants however  the more shade you have the more  ecosystem services that you have so for  instance there's good data that  if you have more birds you're going to  have less coffee bean borer pressure  if you have more shade it's going to  reduce the fluctuations in temperature  and so the leaf rust is going to hit  your coffee less  hard you can also have additional  benefits of nitrogen fixation depending  on the trees that you use  of different soil quality different  nutrient cycling  so there's there's a really strong push  especially from academic circles for  people to grow coffee under some sort of  um agroforest polyculture system  however the um  shift towards growing robusta coffee is  doing the opposite it's pushing for  monocultures because  robusta is a really uh hearty plant  it is completely resistant to breast it  can take harsh conditions it can take  high temperatures  and exposure and it yields  something like twice as much as an  arabica crop  it also has higher caffeine so it's  preferred  for um processed coffee beverages  so your pre-made starbucks coffee  and your four loco and your five hour  energy drink all of those  caffeine products probably come from  robusta  so when we were in mexico in the  southern part of chiapas we met up with  this  lovely professor at ekosur which is the  their regional university  his name is juan barrera and he works on  biological control  of coffee bean borer in robusta  plantations  and one of the things that he showed us  was  the the change so you go from these  beautiful forests to a completely  exposed field with these  enormous robusta plants  usually it's the bigger producers that  are shifting to robusta  because the increase in yield is just  really affordable but he also told us  that  the only buyer in the region is nestle  and that presents a problem for the  farmers because they don't trust nestle  so  there's also been a lot of resistance to  this  and what nestle did was they introduced  four  clones of robusta that they figured were  optimized for production  but this makes the farmers really  skeptical because  they know that they're growing a lower  quality product  which they're not going to be able to  sell if nestle one day decides to leave  or just to not buy their coffee  we did meet this lovely man called fidel  lopez  also in the southern region of chiapas  and he has a completely different story  so  he used to grow conventional coffee and  when the coffee leaf rest hid his farm  in 2012. he decided he just couldn't  afford the pesticides so he was going to  let it go  and basically abandoned his farm for a  couple years and then he came back  and he saw his plans and the way he  tells the story is he found these  trees that just looked more beautiful  than ever  and so he harvested their seeds and  propagated them and started his coffee  plantation again  and he also decided that he was going to  grow  organic coffee because it was safer for  his family  so like i mentioned most of these small  holders have  farms that are maybe  four acres and their farms are on their  their houses are on them and their  children are playing there  so nobody wants to be spraying  pesticides constantly  so for a lot of these small holder  farmers the organic  certification has the appeal that they  feel like they're safer  and that they're taking care of their  little plot of land  he decided also that he was going to  start roasting his own coffee  and selling it and he teamed up with his  family and now the whole family runs  this  little company called cafe richilia  where they sell  the product that they grow and he says  that it's working out really well for  him  because he's actually making more money  and this is him showing us one of those  uh  wonderful trees that he claims has  become resistant to rust  he had at least three different  varieties of coffee  he had one that was produced by the  mexican  equivalent to the usda which was an  improved variety resistant to rust  but the others he had were the  traditional susceptible varieties and he  claims he has  absolutely no rust pressure in his farm  uh so one of the latest things that  is affecting coffee growers is  unfortunately  kovid and this is what the  colombian federation of farmers is doing  to try to keep people safe  they've taken their traditional logo and  transformed it  into on the left to keep social distance  and on the right to cover your mouth  when you sneeze  i was saying that colombia expects to  have  the best harvest ever in 2020  but obviously because  the coffee harvest is so labor intensive  they require a lot of migrant workers to  come in  and they actually move throughout the  whole country so  like i mentioned before coffee  blooms right after the rainy season and  because of the way the winds work in  colombia there's  very discreet rainy season that starts  at the beginning of the year in the  north  and then moves south along the andes  and then ends at the southern part of  the andes in colombia  at around october and then we'll  basically shift the rain  back north so the middle of the country  has two harvests  while the north and the south part have  one and there's this huge  body of migrant workers that just move  with the rains following the coffee  harvest  and obviously covet is a horrible risk  because not only are you putting these  people in risk they're just  literally moving it across  another big problem is that because  people are leaving the countryside for  the cities it's hard to find laborers  and that's one of the  surprising situations in which the very  sad  situation in venezuela has proved useful  for colombian coffee growers and that  venezuelans now make up a big chunk of  the migrant  labors that pick coffee  so anyway um that's all i have  i'm happy to take questions sorry for  the introduction the interruption i  don't even know what happened  but  i know where host is but uh i'm here so  i mean there are the questions for juan  one this is marvin it sounds like with  that narrow genetic diversity  uh the industry's crying out for a  breeding program for  expanding the diversity of the germplasm  so what's happening around the world  with breeding programs um lots of things  there's  well first of all there's uh  there's a lot of people who are  interested in the wild coffee species  in the forest of ethiopia and apparently  there's huge pressure on these forests  um so there's there's a lot of concern  that  the the source of all this genetic  diversity is being lost  because of you know a deforestation an  expansion of  of the agricultural boundaries and all  that stuff um  there is a research group from  texas that is working on producing f1  hybrids  which is something that coffee growers  at least  to my knowledge do not work with um  because because production costs are so  high  what most growers will do is they will  propagate their own plants  so you grab your seeds from your plant  from the good plants and then you  start your new plants if you're working  with an f1 hybrid  you need to you need to buy the seed and  that is going to  mean more costs for the computers so  i know that they're promoting this in  mexico and in guatemala  i haven't heard of an f1 hybrid project  program in colombia um  the fnc which is the colombian  coffee union they have a research  program and they've just released a new  variety  [Music]  they release varieties i think every  five years or so and they are  very very specialized and regionally so  they will  release their latest variety it's called  sunny cafe one  but they have a senica for the centers  and center  for ourselves so it's very specialized  in that sense  um there's also  some people will use rootstock but i've  only seen this in big plantations  you know 300 hectares or more  where they'll use the robusta rootstock  on arabica's scions and that seems to  work really well  for at least for disease pressure  um i know that they've developed um  a transgenic coffee for resistant to  the coffee bean borer but no one wants  to release that because that immediately  means you lose the european market and  no one's gonna buy it so that's a dead  end unfortunately  um yeah that's that's all i know of  thanks any other questions for juana  fascinating look at the  